# The pinned BLIS stack: the ONE source of truth for "which simulator produced these reports".
#
# Read by scripts/build_catalog.py (the `inference-sim` version pill in the site footer) and by
# .github/workflows/generate-report.yml (which clones exactly these refs before running a search).
# SETUP.md documents the same pins for a human setting the skill up by hand.
#
# Shell-sourceable (`set -a; . pins.env; set +a`) and parsed as plain KEY=VALUE lines by Python,
# so keep it to unquoted, comment-or-assignment lines.
#
# Bumping a pin: change it here, then re-run `python3 -I scripts/test_build.py` (it checks this
# file against SETUP.md and the workflow) and rebuild the site.

# inference-sim release whose binary prices every run. BLIS_SHA is the commit the tag pointed at
# when it was pinned; the tag is NOT immutable upstream, so SETUP.md verifies the SHA on clone.
#
# BLIS_SHA is a COMMIT, and it is checked as one. There is deliberately no expected binary digest
# here: the same tag builds to different bytes on different toolchains and platforms (v0.12.0 is
# recorded with two different digests across the committed reports, and gave two more again when
# rebuilt locally), so a pinned digest would be wrong for every contributor but its author. The
# runners resolve the binary's SOURCE commit instead and compare that -- run_search.py's
# blis_pin_warning / _blis_source_commit, guarded by test_search.py and test_build.py. CI refuses
# a moved tag outright (both workflows verify on clone); a local sweep warns, because testing
# against an unreleased simulator is how a pin bump gets evaluated in the first place.
BLIS_REF=v0.12.0
BLIS_SHA=f199223d701d96dddeb1c314509bed003515b599

# WHY THIS PIN IS STILL v0.12.0, and what moving it costs (issue #23).
#
# Upstream is at v0.14.0 (f2ba6bb1, 2026-10-28). The bump is NOT a two-line edit here, because
# v0.14.0 is an architectural change rather than a version bump, and because several lists in
# this repo are hand-transcribed against the pin they were read from. Verified against the
# v0.12.0 and v0.14.0 binaries, both built and run locally:
#
#   * v0.14.0 moves to blis-schemas v0.8.0, where a pool's `engine:` is no longer a fixed struct
#     but an OPAQUE block (deployment.EngineBlock) opened by the engine model that the scenario's
#     engine_version selects -- now blis-vllm v0.32.0's rules/engineconfig.Engine. It also moves
#     to kernel v0.4.0 (KERNEL_REF below).
#   * So ALL 21 names in validate_experiment.ENGINE_NOT_WIRED / run_blis.UNSUPPORTED become real
#     engine fields and their refusals become FALSE. Measured: v0.12.0 fails each at deployment
#     decode ("field watermark not found in type deployment.Engine"); v0.14.0 accepts all 21.
#     Their new behaviour is not uniform, and a bump has to encode the difference:
#       - priced/accepted: watermark, max_num_active_seqs, max_num_scheduled_tokens,
#         stream_interval, kv_cache_memory_bytes, max_loras, custom_ops, use_trtllm_attention,
#         mla_prefill_backend, indexer_kv_dtype, linear_backend, frontend (enum: python|rust),
#         long_prefill_token_threshold_adaptive, decoder_replay_cudagraph_capture_sizes
#       - refused as NOT MODELLED, with an upstream issue: prefix_match_unit (blis-vllm#7),
#         max_cpu_loras (blis-vllm#5), prefix_cache_retention_interval
#       - refused CONDITIONALLY, so they are sweepable only in the right shape:
#         prefill_schedule_interval needs dp>1; disable_hybrid_kv_cache_manager is refused on
#         DeepSeek-V4's sliding-window MLA; env reads only VLLM_USE_V2_MODEL_RUNNER; kv_events
#         is a struct, not a bool.
#   * ENGINE_KNOBS itself is CORRECT as it stands: its 51 names match engineconfig.Engine's 51
#     top-level yaml tags exactly at v0.32.0, in both directions.
#   * run_blis.PD_MUST_MATCH is wrong in both directions against compat/build/pools.go at
#     v0.32.0: it omits mamba_cache_dtype, mamba_ssm_cache_dtype and mamba_cache_mode (all three
#     enforced by requireSameKVCacheFormat), and lists `quantization`, which that function does
#     not compare.
#   * v0.32.0's rules make `max_model_len` MANDATORY ("must be stated; an unstated or auto window
#     is the model's or the memory's"), which every spec that omits it would refuse. That also
#     settles item 3 of #23: the two AgentX specs' `max_model_len: 262144` is still required, and
#     is now required of every spec rather than only those.
#   * Beyond the lists: moving the pin RE-PRICES every committed report. That is 1,453 runs across
#     6 report directories, four of which are AgentX replays at ~21-30 s/run needing the ~569 MB
#     corpus -- so regeneration is hours of compute, not a mechanical edit, and a half-finished
#     bump publishes reports whose `blis:` label contradicts the binary that priced them.
#
# Incidental: qwen3-14b-h100-chatbot-execution gave an IDENTICAL best (1055.99 tok/s/GPU, 127 of
# 156 runs servable) on both binaries, so some reports may survive the bump unchanged -- which is
# worth measuring per report rather than assuming either way.

# blis-latency-kernel, pinned in inference-sim's own go.mod at BLIS_REF (recorded, not cloned).
KERNEL_REF=v0.3.0

# The catalog + registry the pinned binary runs against.
#
# CATALOG_REF was 0.3.0 while the checkout these reports ran against was 0.4.0 -- the `resolved`
# block in every scenario.json recorded the real commit (ba542f0, describe 0.4.0) and that is how
# the mismatch was caught (namasl review). 0.4.0 adds workloads/sharegpt.yaml and changes nothing
# else, so no AgentX number moves; the pin is corrected to the version that actually ran rather
# than re-running against 0.3.0 to match a label.
#
# The registry MUST be v0.2.0 or later:
# it carries the cost-model-memory coefficient set the kernel backend needs AND the acceptance/
# profiles that price speculative decoding (v0.1.1 has cost-model-memory but no acceptance/).
CATALOG_REF=0.4.0
REGISTRY_REF=v0.2.0
